        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x\\ne0$, so division by $x^2$ gives $x^2-10x+26-\\dfrac{10}{x}+\\dfrac1{x^2}=0$. Group the two terms with coefficient $-10$.",
          "worked": "$x=0$ is not a solution because the constant term is $1$. Divide by $x^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{x^4-10x^3+26x^2-10x+1}{x^2}&=0\\\\x^2-10x+26-\\frac{10}{x}+\\frac1{x^2}&=0\\\\x^2+\\frac1{x^2}-10\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)+26&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q62-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p40-q62-b-text-0-0",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Show that this equation is equivalent to",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p40-q62-b-display-1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2-10\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)+24=0,",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p40-q62-b-text-2-0",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "by completing the square of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x^2+\\dfrac1{x^2}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Use $x^2+\\dfrac1{x^2}=\\left(x+\\dfrac1x\\right)^2-2$; hence the constant becomes $24$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2&=x^2+2+\\frac1{x^2}\\\\x^2+\\frac1{x^2}&=\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2-2.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into part (a),\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2-2-10\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)+26&=0\\\\\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)^2-10\\left(x+\\frac1x\\right)+24&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q62-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Reduce the equation to a quadratic by using the substitution $u=x+\\dfrac1x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$u^2-10u+24=0$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $u=x+\\dfrac1x$ into part (b):\n$$u^2-10u+24=0.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 15
      },
      {
        "id": "p40-q62-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Hence, solve $x^4-10x^3+26x^2-10x+1=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=3\\pm2\\sqrt2,\\ 2\\pm\\sqrt3$.\n\nFactorise in $u$ and solve the two quadratics.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}u^2-10u+24&=0\\\\(u-6)(u-4)&=0\\\\u&=6\\text{ or }4.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $u=6$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac1x&=6\\\\x^2-6x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{6\\pm\\sqrt{36-4}}2\\\\x&=3\\pm2\\sqrt2.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $u=4$,\n$$\\begin{align*}x+\\frac1x&=4\\\\x^2-4x+1&=0\\\\x&=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{16-4}}2\\\\x&=2\\pm\\sqrt3.\\end{align*}$$\nAll four roots are nonzero, so the preceding transformations are reversible.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
